window.CourseData=window.CourseData||{};window.CourseData["wisconsin-2023-nec-transition"]={"slug":"wisconsin-2023-nec-transition","title":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC Transition","short":"Wisconsin 2023 NEC Transition","hours":4,"edition":2023,"scope":"wi","audience":"Master, Journeyman, Registered, Residential, Industrial & Inspector","articles":"2023 NEC and Wisconsin SPS 316","free":false,"labs":[],"slideMin":150,"modules":[{"title":"Why Wisconsin Moved to the 2023 NEC: Adoption, Structure and Wisconsin Amendments","minutes":60,"overview":["Why Wisconsin moved from the 2017 NEC to the 2023 NEC","Two revision cycles at once: what 2020 and 2023 mean for Wisconsin electricians","How the NEC is organized and how to read a requirement accurately","Adoption and enforcement: how a model code becomes Wisconsin law","Where to find Wisconsin amendments (SPS 316)","The three Wisconsin-specific updates highlighted by DSPS","Credential categories and how each uses the code","Planning your own transition from 2017 habits"],"slides":[{"tag":"M1·S1","title":"Why Wisconsin Moved from the 2017 NEC to the 2023 NEC","body":"The Wisconsin Department of Safety and Professional Services (DSPS) adopted the 2023 National Electrical Code (NFPA 70), effective September 1, 2026. The 2023 edition replaces the 2017 edition that Wisconsin electricians have been working under. This course helps licensed electricians, registered electricians and inspectors make that transition accurately.","keypoint":"The NEC is a model code. It is written by NFPA, but it has legal force in Wisconsin only because the state adopts it, together with any Wisconsin-specific changes, in its administrative code.","bullets":["Effective date of the 2023 NEC in Wisconsin, per DSPS: September 1, 2026","Edition being replaced: the 2017 NEC","NFPA revises the NEC on a three-year cycle, so editions are numbered 2017, 2020, 2023","DSPS has also stated that it is updating licensing exams","The DSPS press release highlighted three Wisconsin-specific updates: whole-home surge protection, restrictions on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure, and permits and inspections for services on vacant land"],"warning":"This course is a training summary. For the exact legal wording of any Wisconsin requirement, consult the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text. Where this course and SPS 316 differ, SPS 316 controls.","refs":["NEC Art. 90","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M1·S2","title":"Two Revision Cycles at Once: 2017 to 2023","video":{"title":"NEC 2023 Code Changes","dur":"Video"},"body":"Wisconsin is moving directly from the 2017 edition to the 2023 edition. The 2020 edition was not the Wisconsin base edition, so an electrician who studies only the 2023 revision cycle will miss changes that first appeared in 2020. Treat the move as a single jump that spans two revision cycles.","table":{"h":["Topic","2017 NEC","2020 NEC","2023 NEC"],"r":[["Surge protection, general","Arts. 280 (surge arresters) and 285 (SPDs)","Art. 285 continues","Art. 242, Overvoltage Protection, replaces Arts. 280 and 285"],["Surge protection, dwelling services","No service-level requirement","230.67 added for services supplying dwelling units","230.67 continues"],["Emergency disconnect, one- and two-family dwellings","Not required","230.85 added","230.85 continues"],["Energy management systems","No article","Art. 750 added","Art. 750 continues"],["GFCI protection (210.8)","Earlier, narrower scope","Expanded","Further revised"]]},"info":"The table shows only changes this course teaches. It is not a complete list of differences. Use the 2023 NEC itself, and the adopted Wisconsin text, for the full picture.","refs":["NEC Art. 242","NEC 230.67","NEC 230.85"]},{"tag":"M1·S3","title":"How the 2023 NEC Is Organized","video":{"title":"NEC Code Book Overview","dur":"Video"},"body":"Knowing the layout of the NEC is the fastest way to find the right requirement. Article 90 explains the purpose, scope and arrangement of the code. Article 100 holds definitions used in two or more articles. The rest of the code is built from chapters that apply in a set order.","table":{"h":["Part of the NEC","What it contains"],"r":[["Article 90","Introduction: purpose, scope, code arrangement, enforcement, mandatory and permissive rules"],["Chapter 1","General: Article 100 definitions and Article 110 requirements for electrical installations"],["Chapter 2","Wiring and protection: branch circuits, feeders, services, overcurrent protection, grounding and bonding"],["Chapter 3","Wiring methods and materials"],["Chapter 4","Equipment for general use"],["Chapters 5, 6, 7","Special occupancies, special equipment and special conditions; these supplement or modify Chapters 1 through 4"],["Chapter 8","Communications systems; stands alone unless another article specifically references it"],["Chapter 9 and Annexes","Tables; the Annexes are informational and are not enforceable unless specifically adopted"]]},"refs":["NEC Art. 90","NEC Art. 100","NEC 110.3"]},{"tag":"M1·S4","title":"Reading a Requirement Accurately","body":"Most code mistakes come from reading part of a rule. The same method works for any section of the 2023 NEC and prevents the common errors of missing an exception or relying on an informational note.","step_list":["Find the article using the table of contents or the index, then read the scope of the article.","Check the definitions in Article 100 and in Part I of the article for any defined term in the rule.","Read the entire requirement, including every lettered and numbered subdivision under it.","Read the exceptions. An exception changes the rule only for the condition it describes.","Check Chapters 5, 6 and 7, which may supplement or modify the general rule for a special occupancy or equipment.","Treat Informational Notes as explanatory only. They are not enforceable requirements.","Check the Wisconsin adoption in SPS 316 for any amendment to the section before you rely on it."],"keypoint":"Words matter. In the NEC, shall or shall not marks a mandatory rule. Shall be permitted marks a permissive rule: allowed, but not required.","info":"NFPA marks changes in its published editions with symbols, for example an N before new material and a delta symbol before revised text. Read your edition's own explanation of these marks so you can see changes quickly.","refs":["NEC 90.5","NEC Art. 100"]},{"tag":"M1·S5","title":"Adoption and Enforcement: From Model Code to Wisconsin Law","body":"NFPA publishes the NEC, but a code does not bind anyone until a jurisdiction adopts it. In Wisconsin, DSPS adopts the NEC by reference in its administrative code and can add Wisconsin-specific provisions. The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) then enforces the adopted code.","def_grid":[{"term":"Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ)","body":"The organization, office or individual responsible for enforcing a code or standard, or for approving equipment, materials, an installation or a procedure (NEC Article 100)."},{"term":"Adoption","body":"The act by which a jurisdiction gives a model code legal force. Adoption can include amendments, deletions and additions to the model text."},{"term":"Listing and labeling","body":"Equipment must be approved. Listed and labeled equipment, installed according to its instructions, is the normal basis for approval under NEC 110.3."},{"term":"Interpretation","body":"When wording is unclear, the AHJ interprets the adopted code. Ask early, and document the answer you receive."}],"bullets":["The adopted edition and amendments, not the model edition alone, define what is required on a Wisconsin job","Contractors and inspectors must work from the same adopted text","If a project question depends on which edition applies, confirm it with DSPS or the AHJ and consult the current SPS 316 text"],"refs":["NEC 90.4","NEC 110.3","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M1·S6","title":"Where to Find Wisconsin Amendments","body":"Wisconsin-specific rules are not in the NFPA book. The place to find them is the Wisconsin Administrative Code, chapter SPS 316 (Electrical). Always read the NEC section and the SPS 316 text together.","table":{"h":["DSPS-highlighted item","Model code starting point","Where to confirm Wisconsin wording"],"r":[["Whole-home surge protection required","NEC 230.67 and Article 242","Current SPS 316 text"],["Restrictions on how many feeders may supply a building or structure","NEC 225.30 and 230.2","Current SPS 316 text"],["Permits and inspections required for services on vacant land","No NEC equivalent; permitting is a jurisdictional matter","Current SPS 316 text and DSPS guidance"]]},"step_list":["Open the current Wisconsin Administrative Code, chapter SPS 316, from the official Wisconsin legislative or DSPS website.","Locate the provision that corresponds to the NEC section you are working with.","Note whether SPS 316 adds to, changes or removes the NEC requirement.","Record the date you checked, because administrative rules can be revised."],"warning":"Do not rely on summaries, including this course, for details of Wisconsin amendments beyond the three items above. Consult the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text.","refs":["Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M1·S7","title":"Credential Categories and Job Duties","body":"This course applies to every credential category listed for it: electrical master, journeyman, registered, residential, industrial and inspector. Each category uses the code differently, but all of them are held to the adopted edition.","def_grid":[{"term":"Master and journeyman electricians","body":"Plan, install, alter and supervise electrical work. Apply the adopted NEC when selecting equipment, sizing conductors and protection, and designing service and feeder arrangements."},{"term":"Registered electricians","body":"Perform electrical work within the scope of their credential. Need to recognize when a job triggers a new-code requirement and when to ask a supervisor or the AHJ."},{"term":"Residential and industrial electricians","body":"Apply the same adopted code to dwellings or to industrial settings. Dwelling work is where 230.67 surge protection and the GFCI and AFCI rules matter most."},{"term":"Inspectors","body":"Examine installations against the adopted edition and Wisconsin amendments, and explain correction items consistently."}],"bullets":["DSPS has said it is updating licensing exams, so exam preparation material should match the 2023 edition","Credential-specific scope-of-work limits are set by Wisconsin law and rules; consult the current Wisconsin rules for the exact scope of your credential"],"refs":["Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M1·S8","title":"Planning Your Transition and Module Review","body":"A successful transition is mostly preparation. Use the checklist below and then review the module before the knowledge check.","step_list":["Obtain the 2023 NEC (NFPA offers free read-only online access through its website) and mark the articles used in your daily work.","Read the Wisconsin SPS 316 text and note every amendment that touches your work.","Review service and panel details on current and upcoming jobs for surge protection, feeder supply and permit questions.","Confirm with the AHJ which edition governs jobs that straddle the effective date, rather than assuming.","Update estimating templates, material lists and labels to reflect new requirements.","Brief your crew on the three DSPS-highlighted items."],"keypoint":"Module 1 recap: the 2023 NEC became effective in Wisconsin September 1, 2026 and replaces the 2017 NEC. The code is adopted by DSPS, may be amended in SPS 316, and is enforced by the AHJ. DSPS highlighted three Wisconsin-specific updates.","refs":["NEC Art. 90","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"According to DSPS, the 2023 NEC became effective in Wisconsin on:","opts":["January 1, 2023","September 1, 2023","September 1, 2026","January 1, 2027"],"correct":2,"explain":"DSPS adopted the 2023 NEC effective September 1, 2026."},{"q":"The 2023 NEC replaces which edition as the Wisconsin base code?","opts":["The 2017 NEC","The 2008 NEC","The 2014 NEC","The 2020 NEC"],"correct":0,"explain":"Wisconsin is moving from the 2017 edition to the 2023 edition."},{"q":"Because Wisconsin is moving from the 2017 NEC to the 2023 NEC, an electrician should expect:","opts":["Changes from the 2023 cycle only","Only editorial changes with no field impact","No changes except Wisconsin amendments","Changes from two revision cycles, 2020 and 2023, at once"],"correct":3,"explain":"The 2020 edition was not the Wisconsin base edition, so changes first made in 2020 are new to Wisconsin electricians along with the 2023 changes."},{"q":"How often does NFPA publish a new edition of the NEC?","opts":["Every year","Every three years","Every five years","Only when a state requests it"],"correct":1,"explain":"The NEC follows a three-year revision cycle, which is why editions are numbered 2017, 2020 and 2023."},{"q":"Which of the following was NOT one of the three Wisconsin-specific updates highlighted in the DSPS press release?","opts":["Whole-home surge protection","A requirement for AFCI protection in all commercial buildings","Restrictions on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure","Permits and inspections for services on vacant land"],"correct":1,"explain":"The three highlighted items were whole-home surge protection, limits on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure, and permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"q":"Where should you confirm the exact legal wording of a Wisconsin electrical amendment?","opts":["A supplier's product catalog","A summary slide in a training course","The NEC Annexes","The current Wisconsin SPS 316 text"],"correct":3,"explain":"Wisconsin amendments are found in the Wisconsin Administrative Code. Summaries, including training courses, do not replace the adopted text."},{"q":"Chapters 5, 6 and 7 of the NEC generally:","opts":["Supplement or modify the general rules of Chapters 1 through 4 for special occupancies, equipment or conditions","Replace Chapters 1 through 4 entirely","Apply only to utility installations","Apply only to communications circuits"],"correct":0,"explain":"NEC 90.3 arranges Chapters 5 through 7 as supplements or modifications to the general rules in Chapters 1 through 4."},{"q":"Chapter 8 of the NEC (communications systems):","opts":["Is subject to every rule in Chapters 1 through 7","Applies only to buildings over three stories","Stands alone and is not subject to Chapters 1 through 7 except where specifically referenced","Has been deleted from the 2023 NEC"],"correct":2,"explain":"NEC 90.3 states that Chapter 8 is not subject to the requirements of Chapters 1 through 7 except where the Chapter 8 rules specifically reference them."},{"q":"An Informational Note in the NEC is:","opts":["A mandatory rule with penalties","An exception to the rule above it","A Wisconsin amendment","Explanatory material that is not an enforceable requirement"],"correct":3,"explain":"Informational Notes provide explanation. Enforceable requirements are the mandatory and permissive rules of the code text, as described in NEC 90.5."},{"q":"In NEC language, the word shall indicates:","opts":["A suggestion only","A mandatory requirement","A permissive option","A manufacturer recommendation"],"correct":1,"explain":"NEC 90.5 identifies shall and shall not as mandatory rules."},{"q":"In NEC language, the phrase shall be permitted indicates:","opts":["An action that is always required","An action that is prohibited","An action that is allowed but not required","An action that only inspectors may take"],"correct":2,"explain":"Permissive rules say what is allowed. Whether to use the option is left to the installer or designer."},{"q":"Where are terms used in two or more NEC articles generally defined?","opts":["Article 100","Article 90","Chapter 9, Table 1","Annex A"],"correct":0,"explain":"Article 100 contains definitions that apply in two or more articles. Terms used in only one article are defined in that article."},{"q":"The Annexes of the NEC are:","opts":["Informational and not enforceable unless specifically adopted","Always mandatory","Required reading only for inspectors","Limited to Wisconsin amendments"],"correct":0,"explain":"Informational annexes are not part of the requirements of the code unless a jurisdiction specifically adopts them."},{"q":"In NFPA's marked editions of the NEC, a letter N placed before a section generally indicates:","opts":["A note from the NEC committee","A section that applies only to Wisconsin","New material","Material that has been deleted"],"correct":2,"explain":"The N mark identifies new material. Check your edition's explanation of its change marks."},{"q":"The authority having jurisdiction (AHJ) is:","opts":["The manufacturer of the equipment","The organization, office or individual responsible for enforcing the code or approving equipment and installations","The NFPA technical committee","The utility lineman on the job"],"correct":1,"explain":"This is the Article 100 definition of AHJ."},{"q":"Why does the 2023 NEC have legal force on Wisconsin jobs?","opts":["Because NFPA automatically makes it law in every state","Because electricians voluntarily agree to follow it","Because manufacturers require it","Because DSPS adopted it, with any Wisconsin amendments, in the administrative code"],"correct":3,"explain":"A model code is enforceable only after a jurisdiction adopts it. Wisconsin does so through its administrative code, including SPS 316."},{"q":"In addition to adopting the 2023 NEC, what has DSPS said it is doing?","opts":["Eliminating the journeyman credential","Removing permit requirements for services","Updating licensing exams","Publishing a separate Wisconsin electrical code in place of the NEC"],"correct":2,"explain":"DSPS has said it is also updating licensing exams to align with the new code."}],"final":false},{"title":"Surge Protection: Article 242, NEC 230.67 and Whole-Home SPDs","minutes":60,"overview":["What transient overvoltages are and where they come from","What surge-protective devices do and do not do","Article 242, Overvoltage Protection, in the 2023 NEC","SPD Types 1, 2, 3 and 4","NEC 230.67 surge protection for dwelling-unit services","Installation basics for service-level SPDs","Listing, marking and selection","Field scenarios and inspection points"],"slides":[{"tag":"M2·S1","title":"Transient Overvoltages: What They Are and Where They Come From","body":"A transient overvoltage, commonly called a surge or spike, is a very short-duration rise in voltage above the normal system voltage. Surges often last only microseconds, but repeated events can degrade insulation and electronic components, and a large event can destroy them outright.","bullets":["External sources include lightning and utility switching operations","Internal sources include switching of inductive loads such as motors and compressors inside the building","Modern homes contain many sensitive electronic loads: appliances with control boards, HVAC controls, electronics and EV chargers","Repeated small surges can shorten equipment life without any obvious single failure"],"keypoint":"A surge is not the same as a sag, an outage or sustained overvoltage. Surge protection addresses short-duration transients only.","refs":["NEC Art. 242","NEC 230.67"]},{"tag":"M2·S2","title":"What a Surge-Protective Device Does and Does Not Do","body":"A surge-protective device (SPD) is designed to limit transient overvoltages by diverting surge current and clamping voltage. Many SPDs use metal oxide varistors (MOVs), which conduct heavily when voltage rises above a threshold and are otherwise nearly open circuits.","def_grid":[{"term":"What an SPD does","body":"Limits the magnitude of transient voltage on the circuits it protects and diverts surge energy toward the grounding and bonding path."},{"term":"What an SPD does not do","body":"It does not correct sags or outages, does not boost voltage, does not replace overcurrent protection, and cannot guarantee protection from a direct lightning strike."},{"term":"Why bonding matters","body":"An SPD relies on a low-impedance connection to the grounding and bonding system. A poor connection reduces performance."},{"term":"End of life","body":"Components wear out. Most SPDs include a status indicator. A failed indicator means protection may be lost and the device should be replaced."}],"keypoint":"Whole-home surge protection at the service reduces the surges entering from the utility. Point-of-use protection helps with surges generated inside the building. They work best together.","refs":["NEC Art. 242"]},{"tag":"M2·S3","title":"Article 242: Overvoltage Protection in the 2023 NEC","body":"The 2023 NEC consolidates overvoltage protection in a new Article 242, Overvoltage Protection. This article takes over the material formerly found in Article 280 (surge arresters) and Article 285 (surge-protective devices). If you are used to the 2017 NEC, look in Article 242 for the rules you previously found in those articles.","table":{"h":["Topic","2017 NEC","2023 NEC"],"r":[["Surge arresters","Article 280","Article 242"],["Surge-protective devices","Article 285","Article 242"],["Service-level surge protection for dwelling units","Not required","230.67 (introduced in the 2020 edition)"]]},"keypoint":"Update your references. A 2017-era cross-reference to Article 285 now points to Article 242 in the 2023 NEC.","info":"Section numbers inside Article 242 differ from the old Article 285 numbers. Always look up the current section instead of using remembered 285 numbers.","refs":["NEC Art. 242","NEC Art. 280 (2017)","NEC Art. 285 (2017)"]},{"tag":"M2·S4","title":"SPD Types 1, 2, 3 and 4","video":{"title":"NEC 2023 Overview: Schneider Electric","dur":"Video"},"body":"SPDs are classified by type, which tells you where in the system the device may be installed. The type is marked on the device and is part of its listing.","table":{"h":["Type","Where it is installed","Typical use"],"r":[["Type 1","Permanently connected; may be installed on the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device, and also on the load side","Service entrance, meter socket or service equipment"],["Type 2","Permanently connected; installed on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Service equipment and panelboards"],["Type 3","Point of utilization","Cord-connected, direct plug-in and receptacle-type devices"],["Type 4","Component assemblies","Components used inside other listed equipment"]]},"keypoint":"For dwelling-unit services under NEC 230.67, the device must be a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD. A Type 3 plug-in strip does not satisfy that requirement.","refs":["NEC Art. 242","NEC 230.67"]},{"tag":"M2·S5","title":"NEC 230.67: Surge Protection for Dwelling Units","body":"NEC 230.67 requires surge protection at the service for dwelling units. The 2020 edition introduced the requirement, and the 2023 NEC continues it. DSPS has highlighted whole-home surge protection as a Wisconsin update, so this requirement is now enforceable on Wisconsin dwelling services.","bullets":["Applies to services supplying dwelling units","The SPD must be a Type 1 or Type 2 device","The SPD must be an integral part of the service equipment or located immediately adjacent to it; the code includes an allowance when protection is provided at the next level of distribution downstream, so read the exact exception","When service equipment is replaced, the section applies to the replacement","Surge protection is a requirement of the service installation, not an optional accessory"],"keypoint":"An electrician replacing a dwelling-unit service panel in Wisconsin should assume surge protection is part of the job unless the adopted text, as confirmed in SPS 316 and with the AHJ, says otherwise.","warning":"The bullets above summarize 230.67. Before relying on exact wording of the location allowance, the replacement provision or any Wisconsin amendment, consult the 2023 NEC text and the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text.","refs":["NEC 230.67","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"],"checks":[{"q":"A new service supplies a one-family dwelling unit. What is required?","opts":["No SPD","A Type 1 or Type 2 surge-protective device","A GFCI service disconnect","A Type 4 SPD only"],"correct":1,"explain":"All services supplying dwelling units must have a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD.","ref":"NEC 230.67(A) and (C)"},{"q":"Where is the required service SPD for a dwelling installed?","opts":["At the utility pole only","At the farthest receptacle","Integral to the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it","In the attic"],"correct":2,"explain":"The SPD must be an integral part of the service equipment or located immediately adjacent.","ref":"NEC 230.67(B)"}]},{"tag":"M2·S6","title":"Installation Basics for Service-Level SPDs","body":"Follow the manufacturer's instructions and the listing for every SPD. NEC 110.3(B) requires listed and labeled equipment to be installed and used in accordance with its listing and labeling.","step_list":["Select an SPD type and voltage configuration that matches the service, for example a single-phase, three-wire dwelling service.","Confirm that the SPD short-circuit current rating is at least the available fault current at the point of installation.","Choose the mounting method the listing allows: integral to the equipment, a panel-mounted device or an adjacent enclosure.","Provide the overcurrent protection and disconnecting means the manufacturer requires, such as a dedicated breaker.","Keep connecting conductors as short and straight as the instructions allow to keep the let-through voltage low.","Make the grounding and bonding connections exactly as specified.","Verify that the status indicator is visible and working after energizing, and leave the instructions at the panel."],"refs":["NEC 110.3(B)","NEC Art. 242"]},{"tag":"M2·S7","title":"Listing, Marking and Selection","body":"SPDs must be listed. UL 1449 is the product standard under which SPDs are evaluated. The marking on the device tells you whether it is suitable for the installation.","table":{"h":["Marking","What it tells you"],"r":[["Type","Where in the system the device may be installed"],["Maximum continuous operating voltage (MCOV)","Highest steady voltage the device can see without conducting"],["Voltage protection rating (VPR)","The measured voltage let-through when the device is tested with a standard surge; lower is better for the protected equipment"],["Nominal discharge current rating","The surge current level the device is rated to withstand repeatedly"],["Short-circuit current rating (SCCR)","Highest available fault current at which the device may be installed"]]},"warning":"Never install an SPD at a location where the available fault current exceeds the device short-circuit current rating shown on its marking.","refs":["NEC Art. 242","UL 1449"]},{"tag":"M2·S8","title":"Surge Protection in the Field and Module Review","body":"Use the points below when planning a job or checking one. They are also how many inspectors examine the installation.","bullets":["New single-family service: SPD Type 1 or Type 2 present, located in or immediately adjacent to the service equipment, status indicator visible","Panel or service replacement in an existing home: treat surge protection as part of the replacement and confirm the adopted text with the AHJ","Multifamily building with dwelling-unit services: identify which services supply dwelling units","SPD on a dedicated overcurrent device with correct bonding; short conductors","Check available fault current and the device short-circuit rating","Document the installed model and type on the permit or job record"],"keypoint":"Module 2 recap: Article 242 replaces Articles 280 and 285 in the 2023 NEC. NEC 230.67 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling-unit services. Wisconsin has highlighted whole-home surge protection. Always follow the listing and marking.","refs":["NEC 230.67","NEC Art. 242"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"A transient overvoltage is best described as:","opts":["A sustained 10 percent voltage rise lasting hours","A complete loss of voltage","A brief drop in voltage below normal","A very short-duration voltage spike above the normal voltage"],"correct":3,"explain":"Transients last microseconds or less. Sustained overvoltage, sags and outages are different power quality events."},{"q":"Which of the following can generate a damaging surge inside a building?","opts":["Switching of inductive loads such as motors and compressors","Replacing a light bulb","Leaving a door open","Using a battery-powered flashlight"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both external events like lightning and internal events like switching of inductive loads create transients."},{"q":"What is the primary job of an SPD?","opts":["Prevent all power outages","Raise voltage during a sag","Limit transient voltage by diverting surge current","Replace the service overcurrent device"],"correct":2,"explain":"An SPD limits the magnitude of transients. It does not correct sags or outages and does not replace overcurrent protection."},{"q":"Which component is commonly used in SPDs to clamp voltage?","opts":["A fuse link only","A mercury switch","A bimetal strip","A metal oxide varistor (MOV)"],"correct":3,"explain":"MOVs conduct when voltage rises above a threshold, diverting surge current."},{"q":"In the 2023 NEC, overvoltage protection is found in:","opts":["Article 90","Article 242","Article 285","Article 310"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2023 NEC consolidated overvoltage protection into Article 242."},{"q":"Which 2017 NEC articles were consolidated into Article 242?","opts":["Articles 240 and 250","Articles 210 and 215","Articles 280 and 285","Articles 300 and 310"],"correct":2,"explain":"Surge arresters (Article 280) and surge-protective devices (Article 285) now appear in Article 242."},{"q":"A Type 1 SPD differs from a Type 2 SPD in that a Type 1 device:","opts":["May be installed on the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","May only be plugged into a receptacle","Is installed only inside appliances","Does not need to be listed"],"correct":0,"explain":"Type 1 SPDs may be installed on the line side of the service disconnect overcurrent device. Type 2 SPDs are installed on the load side."},{"q":"A Type 2 SPD is installed:","opts":["On the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device","Only in the utility transformer","Only at the point of utilization","On the line side of the utility meter"],"correct":0,"explain":"Type 2 SPDs are permanently connected and installed on the load side of the service disconnect overcurrent device."},{"q":"A cord-connected surge strip is generally which SPD type?","opts":["Type 1","Type 2","Type 3","Type 4"],"correct":2,"explain":"Type 3 SPDs are point-of-utilization devices such as cord-connected and receptacle-type devices."},{"q":"NEC 230.67 applies to:","opts":["Only commercial services","Services supplying dwelling units","Only services over 400 amperes","Only temporary services"],"correct":1,"explain":"230.67 addresses surge protection for services supplying dwelling units."},{"q":"Which SPD types may be used to meet NEC 230.67?","opts":["Type 3 only","Type 4 only","Any plug-in strip","Type 1 or Type 2"],"correct":3,"explain":"230.67 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at the service."},{"q":"Where must the SPD required by 230.67 generally be located?","opts":["Anywhere on the property","Only in the detached garage","As an integral part of the service equipment or immediately adjacent to it","At the last receptacle on each circuit"],"correct":2,"explain":"The location rule is integral to, or immediately adjacent to, the service equipment, subject to the text's exception."},{"q":"When an existing dwelling service equipment is replaced, what does 230.67 require?","opts":["Nothing, because the house is existing","Only a plug-in strip at one receptacle","Only a label on the panel","The surge protection requirements apply to the replacement"],"correct":3,"explain":"230.67 applies when service equipment is replaced. Confirm the adopted wording in the NEC and in SPS 316."},{"q":"Which of the three Wisconsin-specific updates highlighted by DSPS relates to SPDs?","opts":["Permits for vacant land services","Limits on the number of feeders","Whole-home surge protection required","Updated licensing exams"],"correct":2,"explain":"DSPS highlighted whole-home surge protection."},{"q":"An SPD is marked with a short-circuit current rating of 10,000 amperes. Where may it be installed?","opts":["Only where the available fault current is 10,000 amperes or less","Anywhere regardless of fault current","Only where fault current is above 10,000 amperes","Only outdoors"],"correct":0,"explain":"An SPD must not be installed where the available fault current exceeds its marked short-circuit current rating."},{"q":"What is the product standard under which SPDs are listed?","opts":["UL 943","UL 489","NFPA 70E","UL 1449"],"correct":3,"explain":"UL 1449 is the standard for surge-protective devices. UL 943 is for GFCIs and UL 489 for molded-case breakers."},{"q":"To keep SPD let-through voltage low, connecting conductors should be:","opts":["Coiled to add inductance","As short and straight as the manufacturer's instructions allow","As long as possible for flexibility","Spliced in a junction box for easy service"],"correct":1,"explain":"Long or looped leads add impedance and raise the voltage the protected equipment sees."},{"q":"The status indicator on an SPD shows that protection has been lost. What should you do?","opts":["Ignore it because the panel still works","Replace the SPD","Cover the indicator","Add a second circuit breaker"],"correct":1,"explain":"A failed status indicator means the SPD may no longer provide protection and should be replaced."}],"final":false},{"title":"Feeders and Services: Supply Limits, Service Basics, Vacant-Land Permits and Temporary Power","minutes":60,"overview":["Definitions: service, feeder, branch circuit","Service basics under Article 230","Service equipment requirements met daily","Outside branch circuits and feeders, Article 225","Feeder limits: how many may supply a building or structure","Feeder sizing and protection refresher (Article 215)","Permits and inspections for services on vacant land","Temporary power under Article 590"],"slides":[{"tag":"M3·S1","title":"Service, Feeder and Branch Circuit: Definitions That Drive the Rules","body":"Almost every service and feeder rule depends on where a conductor sits in the system. Use the Article 100 definitions to decide which article applies before you size or install anything.","def_grid":[{"term":"Service","body":"The conductors and equipment for delivering electric energy from the serving utility to the wiring system of the premises served."},{"term":"Service point","body":"The point of connection between the facilities of the serving utility and the premises wiring."},{"term":"Feeder","body":"All circuit conductors between the service equipment, the source of a separately derived system, or other power supply source and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device."},{"term":"Branch circuit","body":"The circuit conductors between the final overcurrent device protecting the circuit and the outlet(s)."}],"keypoint":"Where the utility ends and the premises wiring begins is the service point. Utility service requirements are set by the utility and are separate from the NEC and from Wisconsin electrical code requirements, so contact the utility early.","refs":["NEC Art. 100","NEC Art. 230","NEC Art. 215"]},{"tag":"M3·S2","title":"Service Basics: Article 230","body":"Article 230 covers service conductors and equipment. The general rule is that a building or structure is supplied by one service, with limited exceptions.","table":{"h":["Topic","Section","Concept"],"r":[["Number of services","230.2","A building or structure is supplied by only one service unless a listed special condition, special occupancy, capacity requirement or different-characteristics condition applies"],["Service disconnecting means","230.70","Required, in a readily accessible location, outside or inside nearest the point of entrance of the service conductors"],["Maximum number of disconnects","230.71","Not more than six switches or circuit breakers in a single enclosure, group of separate enclosures, or on a switchboard"],["Rating of disconnect","230.79","Minimum ratings by type of occupancy, including a minimum rating for one-family dwellings"],["Surge protection","230.67","Dwelling-unit services (Module 2)"],["Emergency disconnect","230.85","One- and two-family dwelling units (this module)"]]},"refs":["NEC 230.2","NEC 230.70","NEC 230.71","NEC 230.79"]},{"tag":"M3·S3","title":"Service Equipment Requirements Met Daily","body":"Several service requirements affect almost every residential panel installation. The 2023 NEC stacks the surge protection and emergency disconnect requirements that were added in 2020 on top of the long-standing service rules.","bullets":["One-family dwelling service disconnect: the minimum rating is 100 amperes under 230.79","230.85 requires an emergency disconnect for one- and two-family dwelling units, in a readily accessible outdoor location, so first responders can de-energize the building; check the marking and location details in the text","230.67 requires a Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling-unit services","The service disconnecting means must be suitable for use as service equipment, and the grounded conductor and equipment grounding conductors are bonded at the service in accordance with Article 250","The service disconnect count limit (six) applies to the service, so panel and meter-main combinations should be planned with it in mind"],"warning":"Section numbers and exact wording in 230.79, 230.85 and 230.67 should be verified in the adopted text and SPS 316 before you rely on them for a permit or plan submission.","refs":["NEC 230.67","NEC 230.79","NEC 230.85"]},{"tag":"M3·S4","title":"Outside Branch Circuits and Feeders: Article 225","body":"Article 225 covers outside branch circuits and feeders, including feeders that supply a second building or structure on the same property, such as a detached garage or workshop.","table":{"h":["Topic","Section","Concept"],"r":[["Number of supplies","225.30","A building or structure served by a feeder or branch circuit on the load side of a service disconnecting means is supplied by only one feeder or branch circuit unless a special condition applies"],["Disconnecting means","225.31 to 225.33","Each building or structure must have a disconnecting means, with location and a maximum-number limit similar to services (six)"],["Grounding at the second building","250.32","Grounding electrode at each building or structure supplied by a feeder, with an equipment grounding conductor run with the feeder in new installations"]]},"keypoint":"A feeder to a detached building normally carries four conductors in a new installation: two ungrounded, one grounded (neutral) and one equipment grounding conductor. The neutral and ground are not bonded together at the second building.","info":"There are limited exceptions for older installations. Check the text of 250.32 before applying the four-wire rule to an existing building.","refs":["NEC 225.30","NEC 225.31","NEC 225.33","NEC 250.32"]},{"tag":"M3·S5","title":"Feeder Limits: How Many Feeders May Supply a Building or Structure","body":"DSPS highlighted restrictions on the number of feeders that may supply a building or structure as one of the Wisconsin-specific updates tied to the 2023 NEC adoption. The model code starting point is the one-supply rule in 225.30 (and the one-service rule in 230.2). The exact Wisconsin wording is in SPS 316.","table":{"h":["Question","Approach"],"r":[["What is the general rule?","One feeder or branch circuit to a building or structure from a service on the load side of a service disconnecting means; one service per building or structure"],["Does the NEC allow exceptions?","Yes. The code recognizes special conditions such as fire pumps, emergency systems, legally required standby, optional standby, parallel power production systems and multiple sources for enhanced reliability, plus special occupancies, capacity requirements and different characteristics"],["What does Wisconsin add?","DSPS highlights restrictions on the number of feeders; consult the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text for the exact rule"],["What does this mean on a job?","Do not add a second feeder, such as a convenience feeder to a detached building, without confirming that the NEC and SPS 316 allow it"]]},"keypoint":"Convenience, cost savings or equipment layout is not a recognized reason to add a second supply. If you cannot name the specific code condition that permits it, stop and verify.","warning":"This course does not state the Wisconsin-specific feeder limits beyond the DSPS highlight. Consult the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text.","refs":["NEC 225.30","NEC 230.2","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M3·S6","title":"Feeder Sizing and Protection: A Refresher on Article 215","body":"Feeder conductors and their overcurrent protection are sized from the calculated load. Continuous loads, those expected to run three hours or more, require a 125 percent allowance.","bullets":["215.2 sets minimum feeder rating and size: 125 percent of the continuous load plus 100 percent of the noncontinuous load","215.3 sets overcurrent protection: not less than the noncontinuous load plus 125 percent of the continuous load, unless the assembly is listed for operation at 100 percent","Load calculations for dwelling units follow Article 220","EV charging load is included in the calculation (Module 4)","Feeders at 1000 amperes or more on solidly grounded wye systems over 150 volts to ground, not exceeding 1000 volts phase to phase, require ground-fault protection of equipment under 215.10, with exceptions"],"keypoint":"Worked example: a feeder serves 80 A of continuous load and 40 A of noncontinuous load. Minimum feeder ampacity = (80 x 1.25) + 40 = 100 + 40 = 140 A, before applying any adjustment or correction factors.","refs":["NEC 215.2","NEC 215.3","NEC 215.10","NEC Art. 220"]},{"tag":"M3·S7","title":"Permits and Inspections for Services on Vacant Land","body":"DSPS highlighted that permits and inspections are required for services on vacant land. This applies to services set before any building exists, for example a meter pole or pedestal installed in advance of construction. The specific permit procedures, who may obtain a permit, fees and inspection requirements are set by Wisconsin rules and the AHJ. Consult the current Wisconsin SPS 316 text.","step_list":["Before starting, confirm with the AHJ and DSPS guidance that a permit is required for the service you plan to install.","Confirm who is allowed to obtain the permit for your credential.","Confirm the utility's service requirements, which are separate from the electrical code requirements.","Plan the installation to the 2023 NEC as adopted in Wisconsin: service equipment, grounding electrode system, bonding, enclosures and marking.","Schedule the inspection before covering any work that must be inspected.","Confirm with the AHJ and the utility whether energizing is allowed before inspection approval.","Keep the permit and inspection record with the job file."],"warning":"Do not assume a vacant lot is exempt because nothing is built there. Verify the permit and inspection requirements before installing.","refs":["Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316","NEC Art. 230","NEC Art. 250"]},{"tag":"M3·S8","title":"Temporary Power (Article 590) and Module Review","body":"Temporary power is common on construction sites, including sites with a newly set service on vacant land. Article 590 sets the conditions for temporary installations.","bullets":["590.3 allows temporary wiring during construction, remodeling, maintenance, repair or demolition, and for the duration of that work, and it must be removed when the work is done","Temporary wiring for holiday decorative lighting and similar purposes may remain in place for no more than 90 days","590.6 requires GFCI protection for personnel on temporary receptacle outlets; use the 2023 text to confirm which voltages and ampere ratings are covered","Temporary power is not a way around inspection: confirm with the AHJ whether a temporary service or feeder requires a permit under Wisconsin rules"],"keypoint":"Module 3 recap: services and feeders follow the one-supply rule unless a defined condition applies; DSPS highlighted limits on feeders to a building or structure; permits and inspections are required for services on vacant land; consult SPS 316 for exact wording.","refs":["NEC 590.3","NEC 590.6","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"The NEC defines a service as:","opts":["Any circuit that feeds a motor","The conductors between a panelboard and a receptacle","The conductors and equipment for delivering electric energy from the serving utility to the premises wiring system","Only the utility meter"],"correct":2,"explain":"This is the Article 100 definition of service."},{"q":"A feeder is:","opts":["All circuit conductors between the service equipment or other supply source and the final branch-circuit overcurrent device","The conductors between the last overcurrent device and the outlets","The utility conductors at the pole","The grounding electrode conductor"],"correct":0,"explain":"Feeders are the circuit conductors upstream of the final branch-circuit overcurrent device."},{"q":"A branch circuit is:","opts":["The circuit conductors between the final overcurrent device and the outlet(s)","All conductors between the service and the first panelboard","The service lateral","A feeder supplying a second building"],"correct":0,"explain":"This is the Article 100 definition of branch circuit."},{"q":"The service point is:","opts":["The location of the main bonding jumper","The last receptacle on a circuit","The point of connection between the utility facilities and the premises wiring","The grounding electrode"],"correct":2,"explain":"The service point is the boundary between the serving utility and the premises wiring."},{"q":"The general rule under 230.2 is that a building or structure is supplied by:","opts":["Two services","Only one service, unless a permitted condition applies","As many services as the owner wants","One service per floor"],"correct":1,"explain":"230.2 limits a building or structure to one service, with defined exceptions."},{"q":"NEC 230.71 limits the service disconnecting means to not more than:","opts":["Two switches or breakers","Ten switches or breakers","No limit","Six switches or six circuit breakers"],"correct":3,"explain":"230.71 sets a six-disconnect maximum for the service disconnecting means."},{"q":"For a one-family dwelling, 230.79 sets the minimum service disconnect rating at:","opts":["30 amperes","60 amperes","100 amperes","200 amperes"],"correct":2,"explain":"230.79 sets a minimum of 100 amperes for a one-family dwelling service disconnect. Verify the adopted text for your job."},{"q":"NEC 230.85 applies to:","opts":["Industrial motor controllers","Temporary lighting for holidays","Surge arresters over 1000 volts","Emergency disconnects for one- and two-family dwelling units"],"correct":3,"explain":"230.85 requires an emergency disconnect at one- and two-family dwelling units, readily accessible outdoors."},{"q":"Under 225.30, a building served by a feeder on the load side of a service disconnect is generally supplied by:","opts":["Two feeders","As many feeders as the owner wants","Only one feeder or branch circuit","Three branch circuits"],"correct":2,"explain":"The basic rule is one supply, unless a recognized condition permits more."},{"q":"Which of the following highlighted DSPS updates directly concerns feeders?","opts":["Restrictions on how many feeders can supply a building or structure","Permits for services on vacant land","Whole-home surge protection","Updated licensing exams"],"correct":0,"explain":"DSPS highlighted restrictions on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure."},{"q":"Which condition is recognized by the NEC as a reason for more than one supply to a building?","opts":["The owner prefers an extra panel","It saves conductor cost","The new feeder runs in conduit","Fire pumps or emergency systems"],"correct":3,"explain":"Recognized conditions include fire pumps, emergency systems, legally required standby, optional standby, parallel power production and multiple sources for enhanced reliability."},{"q":"The maximum number of disconnects for a building or structure under Article 225 is:","opts":["Three","Six","Twelve","There is no maximum"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 225 uses a six-disconnect limit similar to services."},{"q":"A new feeder to a detached garage in a new installation normally includes:","opts":["Ungrounded conductors only","Ungrounded conductors, a grounded conductor, and an equipment grounding conductor","Two ungrounded conductors and nothing else","A grounded conductor bonded to the garage electrode and no EGC"],"correct":1,"explain":"250.32 calls for an equipment grounding conductor run with the feeder, and the grounded conductor is not bonded to ground at the second building."},{"q":"A feeder serves 80 A continuous and 40 A noncontinuous load. The minimum feeder ampacity before adjustments is:","opts":["120 A","100 A","160 A","140 A"],"correct":3,"explain":"(80 x 1.25) + 40 = 140 A."},{"q":"DSPS highlighted that which of the following is required for services on vacant land?","opts":["Permits and inspections","Nothing, because there is no building","Only a utility sign-off","Only a homeowner affidavit"],"correct":0,"explain":"DSPS highlighted permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"q":"Temporary wiring for holiday decorative lighting may generally remain in place for no more than:","opts":["30 days","180 days","90 days","One year"],"correct":2,"explain":"590.3 allows temporary installations for holiday decorative lighting and similar purposes for a period not exceeding 90 days."},{"q":"Where should you check the exact Wisconsin requirements for vacant-land service permits?","opts":["A supplier's counter staff","A general NEC training video","The NEC Annexes","The current Wisconsin SPS 316 text and DSPS guidance"],"correct":3,"explain":"Permitting is a jurisdictional matter, so the answer is in Wisconsin rules and with the AHJ."}],"final":false},{"title":"Other Common 2017-to-2023 Changes, Field Scenarios and Final Exam","minutes":60,"overview":["GFCI protection expansion","AFCI protection scope","EV charging and Article 625","Solar PV: Articles 690 and 705","Energy storage: Article 706 and related articles","Article 250 highlights for services and buildings","Field scenarios that combine the Wisconsin updates","Final exam preparation"],"slides":[{"tag":"M4·S1","title":"GFCI Protection: Expanding Scope","video":{"title":"NEC 2023 Code Changes","dur":"Video"},"body":"Ground-fault circuit-interrupter (GFCI) protection for personnel has expanded in each revision cycle since 2017. GFCI protection de-energizes a circuit when it detects a small ground-fault current, in the range of about 4 to 6 milliamperes, well below the level that causes a dangerous shock. The main requirements are in 210.8.","table":{"h":["Dwelling-unit location","GFCI protection"],"r":[["Bathrooms","Yes"],["Garages","Yes"],["Outdoors","Yes"],["Crawl spaces at or below grade","Yes"],["Unfinished basements","Yes"],["Kitchens (receptacles serving countertop surfaces)","Yes"],["Within 6 feet of sinks outside kitchens","Yes"],["Bedrooms, living rooms and hallways","Not by 210.8; AFCI protection applies"]]},"info":"The table lists commonly encountered locations only. The 2020 and 2023 editions expanded the voltages, ampere ratings and locations covered by 210.8. Read 210.8 in the 2023 NEC for the complete list and the exact scope.","refs":["NEC 210.8"]},{"tag":"M4·S2","title":"AFCI Protection: Dwelling Units and Beyond","body":"Arc-fault circuit-interrupter (AFCI) protection reduces the fire risk from arcing faults. An AFCI recognizes the electrical signature of an arcing fault and opens the circuit. The main requirements are in 210.12, and the scope has widened across the revision cycles since 2017.","bullets":["Dwelling units: 120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits supplying outlets or devices in rooms such as kitchens, family rooms, dining rooms, living rooms, bedrooms, closets, hallways and laundry areas","Dormitory units and guest rooms and guest suites of hotels and motels are also covered","Where branch circuit wiring in the covered areas is modified, replaced or extended, AFCI protection requirements apply","GFCI and AFCI requirements overlap in places such as kitchens and laundry areas; use a device or combination that provides both when both are required"],"keypoint":"Installers who last worked from the 2017 NEC will find that AFCI and GFCI rules reach more circuits and locations than they remember. Check 210.8 and 210.12 on every job.","refs":["NEC 210.12","NEC 210.8"]},{"tag":"M4·S3","title":"EV Charging and Article 625","video":{"title":"NEC 2023 Overview: Schneider Electric","dur":"Video"},"body":"Electric vehicle supply equipment (EVSE) is installed on many Wisconsin homes. Article 625 covers the electrical equipment and conductors for EV charging. EV charging is treated as a continuous load.","bullets":["Branch circuit and overcurrent device for EVSE are sized at not less than 125 percent of the maximum load of the EVSE","220.57 sets the EV charging load for load calculations at the larger of 7200 watts or the nameplate rating of the EVSE","625.54 requires GFCI protection for personnel for receptacles installed for EV charging","625.43 requires a disconnecting means for EVSE rated more than 60 amperes or more than 150 volts to ground","Energy management systems (Article 750, added in the 2020 NEC) can limit the load of EVSE and other equipment","EVSE must be listed or labeled and installed per instructions"],"keypoint":"If a service upgrade is part of an EV charger job, the new load goes into the Article 220 calculation. The service, feeder and surge protection requirements of this course then apply.","refs":["NEC Art. 625","NEC 220.57","NEC Art. 750"]},{"tag":"M4·S4","title":"Solar PV: Articles 690 and 705","body":"Article 690 covers photovoltaic (PV) systems, and Article 705 covers interconnected electric power production sources. Both have been revised in the 2020 and 2023 cycles.","bullets":["Rapid shutdown in 690.12 applies to PV systems on or in buildings and reduces shock hazard for firefighters by limiting voltage on controlled conductors; the requirements changed between the 2017 and 2020 editions and have continued to evolve","PV disconnecting means and labeling are required so people can identify and isolate the system","Article 705 covers connection to the electrical supply, including supply-side connections ahead of the service disconnect and load-side connections to busbars","For load-side connections to busbars, the rule limits the combined overcurrent devices and inverter output relative to the busbar rating, commonly called the 120 percent rule","Service equipment and PV connections can interact with the surge protection and the service requirements in Modules 2 and 3"],"warning":"Rapid shutdown and interconnection rules are detailed and changed across editions. Use the 2023 text and the adopted Wisconsin text for design and inspection decisions.","refs":["NEC Art. 690","NEC 690.12","NEC Art. 705"]},{"tag":"M4·S5","title":"Energy Storage and Related Articles","body":"Article 706 covers energy storage systems (ESS), which include battery storage systems. Article 706 is a relatively recent addition, introduced in the 2017 NEC. Installers meet ESS on residential solar-plus-storage jobs and increasingly on EV-related projects.","bullets":["Article 706 covers energy storage systems; storage batteries also fall under Article 480","ESS equipment must be listed; for battery ESS this is commonly evaluated under UL 9540","ESS requires a disconnecting means and labeling that identifies the system and its sources","ESS connected to a premises wiring system also falls under Article 705 interconnection rules","Article 710 (stand-alone systems) and Article 750 (energy management systems) were added in the 2020 NEC","Additional ESS siting limits may come from the Wisconsin building and fire codes; consult the AHJ"],"refs":["NEC Art. 706","NEC Art. 480","NEC Art. 705","NEC Art. 750"]},{"tag":"M4·S6","title":"Article 250 Highlights for Services and Buildings","body":"Article 250 has been revised in every revision cycle. Rather than memorizing changes, use this list of Article 250 topics that appear on service and feeder jobs and compare the 2017 and 2023 text for each.","table":{"h":["Topic","Section","What to check"],"r":[["Main bonding jumper","250.28","Connects the grounded conductor and equipment grounding conductors at the service"],["Grounded conductor not reconnected to ground downstream","250.24","The grounded conductor is bonded to ground at the service, not at downstream panels"],["Grounding electrode system","250.50, 250.52, 250.53","Which electrodes are present and how they are installed"],["Grounding electrode conductor sizing","250.66","Size from the service-entrance conductor size"],["Bonding of service equipment","250.92","Methods for bonding service enclosures and raceways"],["Intersystem bonding termination","250.94","Provides a connection point for communications systems bonding"],["Buildings supplied by feeders","250.32","Electrode at each building and equipment grounding conductor with the feeder"],["Equipment grounding conductor sizing","250.122","Size from the rating of the overcurrent device"]]},"keypoint":"A new service on a vacant lot starts with the grounding electrode system. The installation will be inspected, so be ready to show electrodes, conductors and bonding connections before covering.","refs":["NEC Art. 250"]},{"tag":"M4·S7","title":"Field Scenarios: Applying the Wisconsin Updates","body":"Work through each scenario using the code section, the DSPS highlight, and the SPS 316 check.","def_grid":[{"term":"Scenario A: Service replacement","body":"A homeowner needs a new 200-ampere panel on an existing house. What do you plan for? Check 230.67 for an SPD, 230.85 for an emergency disconnect, and Article 250 bonding, then confirm permit and inspection with the AHJ."},{"term":"Scenario B: Pole on vacant land","body":"A client wants a meter pole set now on an empty lot. DSPS highlights permits and inspections for services on vacant land. Confirm the permit and inspection procedure in SPS 316 before work."},{"term":"Scenario C: Second feeder","body":"A contractor proposes a second feeder to a detached garage for convenience. The one-supply rule in 225.30 applies, and DSPS highlights limits on feeders. Identify a recognized condition or do not install."},{"term":"Scenario D: EV charger plus solar","body":"A new 48-amp EVSE and a PV system are added to an existing service. Calculate the load, size the feeder and branch circuit at 125 percent for EVSE, check 705 interconnection rules, and confirm surge protection is in place at the service."}],"refs":["NEC 230.67","NEC 225.30","NEC 625","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]},{"tag":"M4·S8","title":"Final Exam Preparation","body":"The final exam is the knowledge check at the end of this module. It has 18 questions that cover the whole course. You must score at least 70 percent to pass, and the module minimum time must also be met.","step_list":["Review the three DSPS-highlighted updates: whole-home surge protection, limits on feeders to a building or structure, and permits and inspections for services on vacant land.","Review NEC 230.67, Article 242 and the SPD types.","Review the definitions of service, feeder and branch circuit and the one-supply rule.","Review the GFCI, AFCI, EV charging, PV and ESS highlights.","Review the Article 250 table and the field scenarios.","Remember that for details of Wisconsin-specific rules, the answer is to consult the current SPS 316 text."],"keypoint":"If you score below 70 percent you may retake the exam. This course cannot be retaken for credit more than once per credential term.","refs":["NEC 230.67","NEC Art. 242","Wis. Admin. Code SPS 316"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"GFCI protection for personnel is designed to trip at ground-fault currents of about:","opts":["30 to 60 milliamperes","0.5 to 1 ampere","15 to 20 amperes","4 to 6 milliamperes"],"correct":3,"explain":"A Class A GFCI trips when ground-fault current is in the range of about 4 to 6 milliamperes."},{"q":"Which dwelling-unit location is covered by GFCI protection in 210.8?","opts":["Hallways","Living rooms","Garages","Closets"],"correct":2,"explain":"Garages, bathrooms and outdoors are among the locations requiring GFCI protection. Living rooms, hallways and closets are AFCI areas."},{"q":"An AFCI is designed to:","opts":["Detect ground-fault current of 5 milliamperes","Limit voltage surges","Limit short-circuit current","Recognize an arcing fault signature and open the circuit"],"correct":3,"explain":"AFCIs address arcing faults, a fire hazard. GFCIs address shock hazards from ground faults."},{"q":"AFCI protection in dwelling units applies to which circuits?","opts":["Only circuits serving bedrooms","Only outdoor circuits","120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-ampere branch circuits in kitchens, bedrooms, living areas, hallways, laundry areas and similar rooms","Only circuits over 30 amperes"],"correct":2,"explain":"210.12 lists many living areas. The scope is not limited to bedrooms."},{"q":"AFCI requirements in the NEC have been extended over the revision cycles to include:","opts":["Dormitory units and guest rooms and guest suites","Utility transformers","Service entrance conductors","Grounding electrodes"],"correct":0,"explain":"AFCI scope has expanded to additional occupancies including dormitory units and guest rooms and suites."},{"q":"EVSE branch circuits are sized at:","opts":["100 percent of the nameplate","80 percent of the nameplate","50 percent of the nameplate","Not less than 125 percent of the maximum EVSE load"],"correct":3,"explain":"EV charging is a continuous load, so the 125 percent rule applies."},{"q":"For service and feeder load calculations, 220.57 sets the EV charging load at:","opts":["Always 1500 watts","The larger of 7200 watts or the EVSE nameplate rating","Always the breaker size","50 percent of the vehicle battery capacity"],"correct":1,"explain":"220.57 uses the larger of 7200 watts or the nameplate rating of the EVSE."},{"q":"625.54 requires GFCI protection for personnel for:","opts":["Hardwired EVSE disconnecting means only","Receptacles installed for EV charging","Utility meters","Service entrance conductors"],"correct":1,"explain":"625.54 covers receptacles installed for the connection of EV charging."},{"q":"Under 625.43, a disconnecting means is required for EVSE rated:","opts":["More than 5 amperes","Any rating","Only more than 400 amperes","More than 60 amperes or more than 150 volts to ground"],"correct":3,"explain":"625.43 requires a disconnecting means for EVSE rated more than 60 A or more than 150 V to ground."},{"q":"Article 750, added in the 2020 NEC, covers:","opts":["Energy management systems","Overvoltage protection","Large-scale PV","Hazardous locations"],"correct":0,"explain":"Article 750 addresses energy management systems. Overvoltage protection is Article 242."},{"q":"The purpose of PV rapid shutdown under 690.12 is to:","opts":["Increase PV output","Eliminate the need for grounding","Reduce shock hazard to firefighters by limiting voltage on controlled conductors","Replace the service disconnect"],"correct":2,"explain":"Rapid shutdown limits voltage on controlled conductors so first responders face less shock hazard."},{"q":"The '120 percent rule' in Article 705 relates to:","opts":["Maximum voltage drop","Conductor temperature rating","GFCI trip current","The combined overcurrent devices and inverter output compared with the busbar rating"],"correct":3,"explain":"The 120 percent rule limits load-side connection of sources to busbars relative to their rating."},{"q":"Article 706 covers:","opts":["Electrified truck parking","Energy storage systems","Surge arresters","Mobile homes"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 706 covers energy storage systems, including battery storage."},{"q":"The main bonding jumper in a service connects:","opts":["The utility transformer to the meter","The EVSE to the vehicle","The grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductors at the service","The SPD to its breaker"],"correct":2,"explain":"The main bonding jumper is the connection at the service between the grounded conductor and the equipment grounding conductor or enclosure."},{"q":"The intersystem bonding termination in 250.94 provides:","opts":["A point for bonding communications systems to the grounding system","A location for the SPD breaker","The disconnect for EVSE","The main bonding jumper"],"correct":0,"explain":"250.94 provides an accessible termination for bonding communications systems."},{"q":"A contractor is replacing the service equipment on a dwelling. Which requirement from this course should be planned for?","opts":["A Type 1 or Type 2 SPD under 230.67","A Type 3 plug-in strip","Nothing in Article 230 changed","Removal of the grounding electrode"],"correct":0,"explain":"230.67 requires Type 1 or Type 2 SPD at dwelling-unit services, and Wisconsin highlighted whole-home surge protection."},{"q":"A client wants a service set on vacant land before a house is built. What is the first step?","opts":["Install it and ask later","Set it only after the house is complete","Verify permit and inspection requirements with the AHJ and the current SPS 316 text","Skip the grounding electrode until later"],"correct":2,"explain":"DSPS highlighted permits and inspections for services on vacant land."},{"q":"A contractor suggests a second feeder to one detached garage 'for convenience'. What should you do?","opts":["Install it, because more feeders are always better","Verify whether a recognized condition under 225.30 and SPS 316 permits it before installing","Install it and label both","Install it only if cheaper"],"correct":1,"explain":"Convenience is not a recognized condition. DSPS highlighted limits on the number of feeders supplying a building or structure."}],"final":true}]};
